<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: Overview

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
The Main Control Application firmware serves as the main logical control for system operation.

The main control application firmware monitors all the inputs from the various connections on the board hardware. It can also be used to set and control many of the components that the main control board interfaces with. For off-the-shelf components, the main control application can set/enable the motor controller drivers for the cooling system pump and fans, and can enable the ion pump controller.

The Main Control Bootloader firmware allows the system to update the main control application firmware. The Main Control Bootloader firmware runs on the main control MCU of the main control board.

The HVPS Interface Application firmware is used to control and receive feedback from HVPS.

The HVPS interface application firmware reports data back to and receives commands from the main control application firmware. The HVPS interface application firmware contains routines to ramp up the filament current, as well as the high voltage. It contains a PID loop for regulating the emission output to a given setpoint.

The HVPS interface application firmware runs on the MCU of the HVPS interface board.

The HVPS Interface Bootloader firmware allows the system to be able to update the HVPS Interface application firmware. The HVPS Interface Bootloader firmware runs on the MCU of the HVPS interface board.

The Head Interface firmware is used to read various input data from peripherals such as cooling system sensors, magnetometers, and collimator identification. This firmware sends feedback information to the main control application firmware. The head interface firmware can also set various patterns to the head base LED ring for visual indication. The pattern is based on commands received from the main control application firmware. The head interface FW can also enable/disable alignment lasers or illumination LEDs based on membrane keypad inputs.

The Backup Timer firmware is used to interface with the main control firmware and count the registered course of time once prompted. If the set period has elapsed, the timer firmware will trigger the timer MCU to terminate the X-Ray emission.

The Backup Timer firmware runs on the Timer MCUs located on the main control board.

<<<

